<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Duquesne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1754">1754</a>–<a href="/years/1758">1758</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort Duquesne was a French fort built in 1754 at the forks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a>, where the Allegheny and Monongahela rivers meet, on the site of present-day Pittsburgh. Its seizure from Virginian builders helped start the French and Indian War. The French blew it up and abandoned it in November 1758 as a British army under John Forbes approached, and the British built Fort Pitt on the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne.jpg" alt="Fort Duquesne" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sébastien Paquin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fortduquesne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
